'''Uredineæ''' (ū-rē-din′ẹ̄-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (Brongniart, 1824), < ''Uredo'' (''-din-'') + ''-eæ.''] An order of minute ascomycetous fungi, parasitic chiefly upon living flowering plants and ferns, and frequently very injurious to them. {{smaller|It includes the forms known as ''rust, smut, mildew'', etc. The order is remarkable for the peculiar alternation of forms undergone by many of the species, which are known as the æcidium form, uredoform, and teleutoform, and which were long considered as independent genera. ''Puccinia graminis'', the so-called corn-mildew, may be taken as the type of the course of development followed by most ''Uredineæ'', the three form-genera ''Æcidium, Uredo'', and ''Puccinia'' being different stages of it. The first or æcidium stage is the cluster-cup of the barberry; the second or uredoform is the red-rust of grain; and the third or ''Puccinia'' is the mature form. See ''Fungi, Puccinia, rust''<sup>1</sup>, 3, ''mildew, Micropuccinia, Coniomycetes, heterœcism.''—'''Tremelloid Uredineæ''', a group of ''Uredineæ'' which do not possess a sporocarp generation, but consist of a teleutospore-bearing generation with usually softer and more gelatinous membranes.}}

'''uredineous''' (ū-rē-din′ẹ̄-us), ''a.'' [< ''Uredineæ'' + ''-ous.''] '''1.''' Of or pertaining to the ''Uredineæ.''—'''2.''' Affected by uredo.

'''Uredines''' (ū-red′i-nēz), ''n. pl.'' [NL., pl. of ''Uredo.''] In ''bot.'', the ''Uredineæ.''

'''uredinoid''' (ū-red′i-noid), ''a.'' In ''bot.'', resembling the ''Uredineæ'', or having their characters.

'''uredinous''' (ū-red′i-nus), ''a.'' Same as ''uredineous.''

'''Uredo''' (ū-rē′dō), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''uredo'', a blight, a blast, < ''urere'' (√ ''us''), kindle, burn: see ''ustion.''] '''1.''' A form-genus or stage in the development of fungi of the order ''Uredineæ.'' {{smaller|It is the stage}}

